window.PREP_DATA = window.PREP_DATA || {};
window.PREP_DATA.react = {
  notes: [
    {
      "title": "UI = f(state)",
      "body": "React draws the screen from props and state. Props are inputs from the parent. State is data this component remembers. You describe what the UI should look like. React updates the real DOM after data changes. You do not move DOM nodes by hand for every click."
    },
    {
      "title": "Components",
      "body": "A component is a function that returns JSX, which looks like HTML inside JavaScript. It receives props as its argument. Keep components small so one file does one job. When two siblings need the same data, move that data to their parent. That is called lifting state up."
    },
    {
      "title": "Hooks rules",
      "body": "A hook is a function such as useState that lets a component use React features. Call hooks only at the top of a React function, not inside if or loops. React matches hooks by order: first call is slot 1, second is slot 2. Custom hooks are functions whose names start with use. They can call other hooks."
    },
    {
      "title": "useState",
      "body": "useState returns the current value and a setter. Calling the setter schedules a new render. The new value is not on the next line yet. If the next value depends on the previous one, use setX(x => x + 1). React may group several setters into one paint."
    },
    {
      "title": "useEffect",
      "body": "useEffect runs after React has painted. Use it to sync with something outside React: network, timers, or subscriptions. Return a cleanup function to stop that work. List every value from the component that the effect reads. Missing dependencies cause stale data."
    },
    {
      "title": "Keys",
      "body": "A key is a stable name on each item in a list. React uses keys to match old rows to new rows. Use an id from your data. Index keys break when the list reorders, because item 0 is then a different person. Wrong keys make input state jump to the wrong row."
    },
    {
      "title": "Props vs state",
      "body": "Props come from the parent and are read-only. State is owned by this component and can change. Changing either can make React draw again. If a value can be computed from props or state, compute it during render. Do not copy it into extra state."
    },
    {
      "title": "Re-renders",
      "body": "A state change re-renders that component and its children by default. A parent render also re-renders children. memo can skip a child if props look the same. useMemo and useCallback remember values and functions. Use them when you measured a real cost, not on every line."
    },
    {
      "title": "Controlled inputs",
      "body": "A controlled input keeps its value in React state. You pass value and onChange. That is the usual form pattern. An uncontrolled input keeps the value in the DOM. You read it with a ref when you need it. Controlled inputs make validation easier."
    },
    {
      "title": "Context",
      "body": "Context passes data through the tree without a long chain of props. You create a context, wrap a Provider, and read with useContext. Consumers re-render when the value changes. If you pass a new object every render, everyone updates. Split contexts if one value changes too often."
    },
    {
      "title": "Router",
      "body": "A router maps URLs to components. React Router is a common library for that. Use Link for in-app moves so the whole page does not reload. Nested routes keep a layout on screen while the inner page changes. Loaders can fetch data near the page that needs it."
    },
    {
      "title": "Clear UI talk",
      "body": "Explain that React compares a description of the UI to the last description, then updates the DOM. Mention when you would skip a render with memo. Use real HTML: labels, buttons, and headings. Keyboard users and screen readers need those tags. Fancy hooks come after the screen is correct."
    }
  ],
  questions: [
    { id: 1, level: "beginner", q: "What is React?", a: "React is a library for building website screens from small pieces called components. You write how the screen should look. React updates the page when data changes.\n\nIt is not a whole website by itself. You still need HTML and JavaScript. A component is a function that returns markup.\n\nIn the code: Hello is a function component. It returns an h1 that says Hello. React draws Hello onto the page. There is no extra state yet.\n\nUse React when the screen has many pieces that change over time.", code: "// a tiny piece of screen\nfunction Hello() { // a reusable step\n  return <h1>Hello</h1>;  // what to show\n}\n// React draws Hello onto the page" },
    { id: 2, level: "beginner", q: "What is JSX?", a: "JSX looks like HTML written inside JavaScript. It is not HTML. A compiler turns it into function calls. You put JavaScript values inside curly braces { }.\n\nAlmost every React file uses JSX. class becomes className because class is a reserved word in JavaScript.\n\nIn the code: name is \"Ada\". Title returns an h1. {name} inserts Ada into the heading. The braces are how JS values enter the markup.\n\nA common mistake is wrapping a JS value in quotes inside JSX. Use {name}, not \"{name}\".", code: "const name = \"Ada\"; // a named box\nfunction Title() { // a reusable step\n  // { } drops a JS value into the markup\n  return <h1>Hello, {name}</h1>; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 3, level: "beginner", q: "What is a component?", a: "A component is a reusable chunk of UI. A function component takes props (inputs) and returns JSX. It can remember state and run effects.\n\nKeep components small so they are easy to reuse. The parent passes props as attributes.\n\nIn the code: Badge receives { label } and shows it in a span. Page renders Badge with label=\"New\". The same Badge can be reused with a different label.\n\nIf a piece of UI appears twice, make it a component instead of copying JSX.", code: "function Badge({ label }) { // a reusable step\n  // label is a prop from the parent\n  return <span className=\"badge\">{label}</span>; // send this back\n}\nfunction Page() { // a reusable step\n  return <Badge label=\"New\" />;  // reuse\n}" },
    { id: 4, level: "beginner", q: "props vs state?", a: "Props are inputs handed down by the parent. You should not change them. State is data this component owns and can change over time.\n\nChanging either can make React draw the screen again. Know who is allowed to edit what.\n\nIn the code: start is a prop. n is state from useState(start). The button shows n and calls setN(n + 1) on click. The comment notes that start came from props.\n\nDo not assign to props. Call a setter the parent gave you, or use your own state.", code: "function Counter({ start }) { // a reusable step\n  const [n, setN] = useState(start); // state we own\n  return ( // send this back\n    <button onClick={() => setN(n + 1)}>\n      {n} {/* start came from props */}\n    </button>\n  );\n}" },
    { id: 5, level: "beginner", q: "What is a hook?", a: "A hook is a function that lets a function component use React features. useState remembers a value. useEffect talks to the outside world.\n\nCustom hooks are just functions whose names start with use. Hooks replaced most class-component patterns.\n\nIn the code: Box calls useState(false) to get on and setOn. The button flips on with setOn(!on). The label shows On or Off.\n\nCall hooks only inside React function components or other hooks.", code: "function Box() { // a reusable step\n  const [on, setOn] = useState(false); // a hook\n  return ( // send this back\n    <button onClick={() => setOn(!on)}>\n      {on ? \"On\" : \"Off\"}\n    </button>\n  );\n}" },
    { id: 6, level: "beginner", q: "Why must hooks stay at the top level?", a: "React matches hook calls by order: first call is slot 1, second is slot 2. If you skip a hook some renders, the slots mix up and state breaks.\n\nAlways call the same hooks in the same order. Do not put hooks inside if.\n\nIn the code: useState is always first. The if (vip) useEffect line is commented as BAD. useEffect(() => {}, [vip]) is always called. The paragraph shows n.\n\nIf you need a hook to depend on vip, pass vip in the dependency list. Do not skip the call.", code: "function Card({ vip }) { // a reusable step\n  const [n, setN] = useState(0); // always first\n  // if (vip) useEffect(...);  // BAD: sometimes skipped\n  useEffect(() => {}, [vip]);    // OK: always called\n  return <p>{n}</p>; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 7, level: "beginner", q: "How does useState work at a high level?", a: "useState returns the current value and a setter function. React stores that value on this component. When you call the setter, React plans a new render.\n\nThe first argument is the starting value. After a render, you read the new value from the hook.\n\nIn the code: likes starts at 0. The button shows \"{likes} likes\". onClick calls setLikes(likes + 1). Each click schedules a render with a bigger number.\n\nDo not expect likes to change on the next line inside onClick. Wait for the next render.", code: "function Like() { // a reusable step\n  const [likes, setLikes] = useState(0); // start at 0\n  return ( // send this back\n    <button onClick={() => setLikes(likes + 1)}>\n      {likes} likes\n    </button>\n  );\n}" },
    { id: 8, level: "beginner", q: "Why is setState asynchronous / batched?", a: "React may wait and group several updates into one paint. That avoids extra work. Right after setCount, count is still the old number.\n\nUse the updater function if the next value depends on the last one. Use an effect if you need to run after the new value is on screen.\n\nIn the code: addTwo calls setN((x) => x + 1) twice. Each updater sees the latest pending value, so n grows by 2, not 1. The button shows n.\n\nIf you write setN(n + 1) twice with the same n, you might only add 1.", code: "function Demo() { // a reusable step\n  const [n, setN] = useState(0); // remember a value\n  function addTwo() { // a reusable step\n    setN((x) => x + 1); // use latest pending value\n    setN((x) => x + 1); // becomes +2, not +1\n  }\n  return <button onClick={addTwo}>{n}</button>; // when the user acts\n}" },
    { id: 9, level: "beginner", q: "What does the useState updater function do?", a: "The updater form is setCount(c => c + 1). React passes you the latest pending state. You return the next state.\n\nUse this when the next number depends on the last number, especially if you call setState twice quickly.\n\nIn the code: plus uses setN((c) => c + 1). c is the latest count, not a stale copy from the render. The button shows n and calls plus.\n\nPrefer the updater whenever you write “add one” or “toggle.”", code: "function Score() { // a reusable step\n  const [n, setN] = useState(0); // remember a value\n  function plus() { // a reusable step\n    setN((c) => c + 1); // c is the latest count\n  }\n  return <button onClick={plus}>{n}</button>; // when the user acts\n}" },
    { id: 10, level: "beginner", q: "What is a key and why not use index?", a: "A key is a name tag so React can match old rows to new rows. Index keys break when the list reorders, because item 0 is a different person now.\n\nPrefer a stable id from your data. Wrong keys make input state jump to the wrong row.\n\nIn the code: users.map renders an li for each user. key={u.id} uses a stable id, not the array index. u.name is the visible text.\n\nNever use index as a key if rows can insert, delete, or move.", code: "function List({ users }) { // a reusable step\n  return users.map((u) => ( // make a new list\n    <li key={u.id}>     {/* stable id, not index */}\n      {u.name}\n    </li>\n  )); // shows the idea\n}" },
    { id: 11, level: "beginner", q: "What is the virtual DOM?", a: "The virtual DOM is a JavaScript description of the UI tree, not the real page yet. React compares the next tree with the last one. Then it only changes the real DOM pieces that need it.\n\nToday React also uses Fiber to schedule work. You still write components that return JSX.\n\nIn the code: App returns a p with Hello and name. If name changes, React patches the text node. The JSX is the virtual tree first.\n\nYou do not update document.querySelector by hand for this pattern. Let React diff the trees.", code: "function App({ name }) { // a reusable step\n  // this JSX becomes a virtual tree, then DOM updates\n  return <p>Hello {name}</p>; // send this back\n}\n// if name changes, React patches the text node" },
    { id: 12, level: "beginner", q: "What is reconciliation?", a: "Reconciliation is how React decides what to update. It compares old and new trees. Same component type and key can reuse the running instance.\n\nA different type throws the old one away and starts fresh. Swapping tags or keys by accident resets state.\n\nIn the code: Box returns h1 or p based on asTitle. Changing type remounts children. The text is still Hi, but it is a new element type.\n\nKeep the same tag type if you want inner state to survive.", code: "function Box({ asTitle }) { // a reusable step\n  // changing type remounts children\n  return asTitle ? <h1>Hi</h1> : <p>Hi</p>; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 13, level: "beginner", q: "Controlled vs uncontrolled component?", a: "Controlled means React state is the source of truth, with value and onChange. Uncontrolled means the DOM holds the value, and you read it with a ref.\n\nForms are usually controlled so validation is easy. The screen then always matches your data.\n\nIn the code: name lives in useState. The input has value={name} and onChange that calls setName(e.target.value). Every keystroke updates React, then the input.\n\nDo not mix value={name} with an input that also keeps its own DOM value.", code: "function NameField() { // a reusable step\n  const [name, setName] = useState(\"\"); // React owns it\n  return ( // send this back\n    <input\n      value={name}\n      onChange={(e) => setName(e.target.value)} // when the user acts\n    />\n  );\n}" },
    { id: 14, level: "beginner", q: "What is lifting state up?", a: "Lifting state up means moving shared data to the closest parent both children can see. Pass the data and the setter down as props. Then there is only one copy of the truth.\n\nUse this when two boxes must stay in sync.\n\nIn the code: Parent owns n. Child receives value={n}. The button in Parent calls setN(n + 1). Both children would see the same n if you passed it to each.\n\nIf two siblings each have their own useState for the same fact, they will drift apart.", code: "function Parent() { // a reusable step\n  const [n, setN] = useState(0); // shared here\n  return ( // send this back\n    <>\n      <Child value={n} />\n      <button onClick={() => setN(n + 1)}>+</button>\n    </>\n  );\n}" },
    { id: 15, level: "beginner", q: "children prop?", a: "Nested JSX arrives as props.children. Layout wrappers use this a lot. You can put text, elements, or more components there.\n\nCards and modals take children so the wrapper does not know the inner content.\n\nIn the code: Card renders a div.card around {children}. Page puts a p inside Card. Hello inside is the child.\n\nIf a component only exists to wrap markup, take children instead of many one-off props.", code: "function Card({ children }) { // a reusable step\n  return <div className=\"card\">{children}</div>; // send this back\n}\nfunction Page() { // a reusable step\n  return <Card><p>Hello inside</p></Card>; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 16, level: "intermediate", q: "When does a component re-render?", a: "A component re-renders when its own state changes, when context it reads changes, or when a parent re-renders. By default children re-render with the parent.\n\nmemo can skip a child if props look the same (shallow compare). Profile a slow page before you memo everything.\n\nIn the code: Child logs \"child render\" and shows n. Parent has its own n and a button. The snippet shows Parent’s button; a real tree would render Child too, and Parent’s setN would redraw children.\n\nIf a child is slow, measure first, then memo.", code: "function Child({ n }) { // a reusable step\n  console.log(\"child render\"); // print to see it\n  return <p>{n}</p>; // send this back\n}\nfunction Parent() { // a reusable step\n  const [n, setN] = useState(0); // remember a value\n  return <button onClick={() => setN(n + 1)}>{n}</button>; // when the user acts\n}" },
    { id: 17, level: "intermediate", q: "useEffect dependency array?", a: "The second argument of useEffect is the dependency array. [] means run after first paint, cleanup on unmount. No array means run after every render.\n\n[a, b] means run when a or b change (Object.is). Wrong deps cause stale data or extra fetches.\n\nIn the code: the first effect sets document.title when name changes. The second logs \"mounted\" once, and \"unmounted\" on cleanup, because the array is [].\n\nList every value from the component that the effect reads.", code: "useEffect(() => { // run after paint\n  document.title = name;   // sync the tab title\n}, [name]);                // only when name changes\nuseEffect(() => { // run after paint\n  console.log(\"mounted\"); // print to see it\n  return () => console.log(\"unmounted\"); // print to see it\n}, []);                    // once" },
    { id: 18, level: "intermediate", q: "What belongs in useEffect vs event handlers?", a: "Effects sync with something outside React: network, timers, subscriptions. A click belongs in onClick, not in an effect. Do not fetch in render.\n\nPutting user actions in effects causes extra runs and confusing bugs.\n\nIn the code: onClick calls save() when the user clicks. useEffect starts setInterval(ping) and clears it on cleanup. The button uses onClick={onClick}.\n\nIf the user did it, use an event handler. If the outside world must stay in sync, use an effect.", code: "function Save() { // a reusable step\n  function onClick() { // when the user acts\n    save();                // user action → handler\n  }\n  useEffect(() => { // run after paint\n    const id = setInterval(ping, 10000); // outside world\n    return () => clearInterval(id); // send this back\n  }, []); // shows the idea\n  return <button onClick={onClick}>Save</button>; // when the user acts\n}" },
    { id: 19, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you clean up an effect?", a: "Return a function from useEffect. React runs it before the next effect and when the component unmounts. Clear timers, abort fetches, unsubscribe.\n\nThat way you do not update state on a component that is gone.\n\nIn the code: the effect starts setInterval and saves id. The return function calls clearInterval(id). The dependency list is [], so this is mount and unmount.\n\nA missing cleanup is a common leak in React apps.", code: "useEffect(() => { // run after paint\n  const id = setInterval(() => { // run later\n    console.log(\"tick\"); // print to see it\n  }, 1000); // shows the idea\n  return () => clearInterval(id); // cleanup\n}, []); // shows the idea" },
    { id: 20, level: "intermediate", q: "What is a stale closure?", a: "A stale closure means a function captured props or state from an old render. Usually a missing dependency, or an interval created once.\n\nFix the deps, use a ref for the latest value, or the setState updater.\n\nIn the code: Tick starts an interval in an effect with []. The interval calls setN((x) => x + 1), so it does not need the old n. Cleanup clears the interval. The p shows n.\n\nIf you wrote setN(n + 1) inside that interval, n would stay 0 forever.", code: "function Tick() { // a reusable step\n  const [n, setN] = useState(0); // remember a value\n  useEffect(() => { // run after paint\n    const id = setInterval(() => { // run later\n      setN((x) => x + 1); // updater avoids stale n\n    }, 1000); // shows the idea\n    return () => clearInterval(id); // send this back\n  }, []); // shows the idea\n  return <p>{n}</p>; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 21, level: "intermediate", q: "useRef vs useState?", a: "useRef holds .current that you can change without a re-render. Great for DOM nodes and values that should not redraw the screen. useState is for values that should show on screen.\n\nDo not re-render on every mouse pixel if a ref is enough.\n\nIn the code: el is a ref. focus() calls el.current.focus(). The input has ref={el} and onClick={focus}. Focusing does not need a state update.\n\nUse a ref for the DOM node. Use state for the text the user should see.", code: "function Input() { // a reusable step\n  const el = useRef(null);     // DOM box\n  function focus() { // a reusable step\n    el.current.focus();        // no re-render needed\n  }\n  return <input ref={el} onClick={focus} />; // when the user acts\n}" },
    { id: 22, level: "intermediate", q: "useMemo vs useCallback?", a: "useMemo remembers a computed value. useCallback remembers a function. useCallback is useMemo for functions. Both need a dependency list.\n\nMeasure first. Do not sprinkle them everywhere.\n\nIn the code: total is memoized from items.reduce. onSave is a useCallback that calls save(total) when total changes. The deps are [items] and [total].\n\nMemoize a callback only if a memoized child actually needs a stable function.", code: "const total = useMemo(() => items.reduce((s, x) => s + x, 0), [items]); // a named box\nconst onSave = useCallback(() => { // a named box\n  save(total); // shows the idea\n}, [total]); // shows the idea" },
    { id: 23, level: "intermediate", q: "React.memo?", a: "memo wraps a component and skips if props are shallow-equal. The child still re-renders if it has its own state. A custom compare is easy to get wrong.\n\nUse it for expensive rows in a list.\n\nIn the code: Row is memoized and shows name. List maps names to Row with key={n} and name={n}. If name is the same, Row can skip.\n\nmemo does nothing useful if you pass a new object or inline function every time.", code: "const Row = memo(function Row({ name }) { // a named box\n  return <li>{name}</li>;     // skip if name is the same\n}); // shows the idea\nfunction List({ names }) { // a reusable step\n  return names.map((n) => <Row key={n} name={n} />); // make a new list\n}" },
    { id: 24, level: "intermediate", q: "What is context?", a: "Context passes data without a long prop chain. createContext, wrap a Provider, read with useContext. Consumers re-render when the value changes.\n\nIf you pass a new object every render, everyone updates. Memoize the value if needed. Theme and auth are typical uses.\n\nIn the code: Theme defaults to \"light\". App provides \"dark\". Button reads t with useContext(Theme) and shows it. The button text is dark.\n\nDo not put fast-changing mouse coordinates in a wide context.", code: "const Theme = createContext(\"light\"); // a named box\nfunction App() { // a reusable step\n  return ( // send this back\n    <Theme.Provider value=\"dark\">\n      <Button />\n    </Theme.Provider>\n  );\n}\nfunction Button() { // a reusable step\n  const t = useContext(Theme); // a named box\n  return <button>{t}</button>; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 25, level: "intermediate", q: "Prop drilling?", a: "Prop drilling is passing props through layers that do not use them. Parent → A → B → C just to get data to C is noisy.\n\nFix with children composition, context, or a store for truly global data. As apps grow, this gets painful.\n\nIn the code: Page takes user but only Hello needs user.name. Layout does not have to know user. The comment shows the old drill through A and B.\n\nPass props only to who needs them. Use context when many distant children need the same value.", code: "// drilling: <A user={user} /> then <B user={user} />\nfunction Page({ user }) { // a reusable step\n  return ( // send this back\n    <Layout>\n      <Hello name={user.name} /> {/* pass only to who needs it */}\n    </Layout>\n  );\n}" },
    { id: 26, level: "intermediate", q: "What is composition in React?", a: "Composition means nesting components and passing children or slots. Do not make ComponentB extend ComponentA. React is “has-a,” not “is-a.”\n\nBuild screens from pieces instead of class inheritance.\n\nIn the code: Shell takes sidebar and children. aside shows sidebar. main shows children. You assemble a page by passing those slots.\n\nPrefer a Shell with slots over a BasePage class to extend.", code: "function Shell({ sidebar, children }) { // a reusable step\n  return ( // send this back\n    <div>\n      <aside>{sidebar}</aside>\n      <main>{children}</main>\n    </div>\n  );\n}" },
    { id: 27, level: "intermediate", q: "Fragments?", a: "A fragment <> </> groups children with no extra DOM node. In lists, use <Fragment key={id}>. That keeps HTML valid, such as inside tables, and avoids extra wrappers that break CSS.\n\nUse fragments when you need two siblings and no extra div.\n\nIn the code: Pair returns a fragment with dt and dd. There is no wrapping div. Name and Ada stay as sibling tags.\n\nIf CSS expects a direct child, a wrapper div from React can break the layout. Use a fragment.", code: "function Pair() { // a reusable step\n  return ( // send this back\n    <>\n      <dt>Name</dt>\n      <dd>Ada</dd>\n    </>\n  );\n}" },
    { id: 28, level: "intermediate", q: "Portals?", a: "A portal draws children in another DOM node, such as document.body. Modals and tooltips use this so overflow and z-index work. Events still bubble in the React tree.\n\nUse a portal when a CSS parent would clip the dialog.\n\nIn the code: Modal calls createPortal with an overlay div and document.body. children go inside the overlay. React still owns the tree, but the DOM parent is body.\n\nIf a modal is cut off by overflow: hidden, portal it to body.", code: "function Modal({ children }) { // a reusable step\n  return createPortal( // send this back\n    <div className=\"overlay\">{children}</div>,\n    document.body              // draw here, not inside parent\n  );\n}" },
    { id: 29, level: "intermediate", q: "What are synthetic events?", a: "Synthetic events are React’s wrapped event objects, so browsers behave more alike. In modern React they delegate at the root. Call e.preventDefault() as usual.\n\nYou write one event style for clicks, submits, and more.\n\nIn the code: onSubmit calls e.preventDefault() so the page does not reload. Then it logs \"save\". The form uses onSubmit={onSubmit}.\n\nAlways preventDefault on submit if you handle the form in JavaScript.", code: "function Form() { // a reusable step\n  function onSubmit(e) { // a reusable step\n    e.preventDefault();        // stop page reload\n    console.log(\"save\"); // print to see it\n  }\n  return <form onSubmit={onSubmit}><button>Go</button></form>; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 30, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you handle forms?", a: "Keep each field in state, or one object of fields. Listen to onChange, validate on submit or on blur, and preventDefault. Big forms often use a library.\n\nYou control what gets sent.\n\nIn the code: email is state. onSubmit prevents default and logs email. The input is controlled with value and onChange. The form wraps the input.\n\nDo not forget preventDefault, or the browser will reload the page.", code: "function Login() { // a reusable step\n  const [email, setEmail] = useState(\"\"); // remember a value\n  function onSubmit(e) { // a reusable step\n    e.preventDefault(); // shows the idea\n    console.log(email); // print to see it\n  }\n  return ( // send this back\n    <form onSubmit={onSubmit}>\n      <input value={email} onChange={(e) => setEmail(e.target.value)} />\n    </form>\n  );\n}" },
    { id: 31, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you fetch data in React?", a: "A common pattern is useEffect plus loading, error, and empty states. Libraries like React Query help cache and retry. Router loaders can fetch before paint.\n\nAbort when the id changes so an old response does not win.\n\nIn the code: User stores user in state. The effect fetches \"/u/\" + id, then setUser if stop is still false. Cleanup sets stop = true. The p shows user?.name or a placeholder.\n\nDo not fetch in the function body. Put it in an effect or a data library.", code: "function User({ id }) { // a reusable step\n  const [user, setUser] = useState(null); // remember a value\n  useEffect(() => { // run after paint\n    let stop = false; // a named box\n    fetch(\"/u/\" + id).then((r) => r.json()).then((u) => { // ask the network\n      if (!stop) setUser(u); // pick a path\n    }); // shows the idea\n    return () => { stop = true; }; // send this back\n  }, [id]); // shows the idea\n  return <p>{user?.name || \"…\"}</p>; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 32, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you avoid a race in fetch?", a: "If id changes quickly, an old slow response can arrive after a new one. Ignore stale responses with a flag, a request id, or AbortController.\n\nOnly show data for the latest id. Otherwise Ada’s profile can flash Bob’s data.\n\nIn the code: AbortController is created per effect. fetch uses the signal. then parses json and setUser. Cleanup calls ctrl.abort(). The dep is [id].\n\nAbort the previous fetch whenever id changes.", code: "useEffect(() => { // run after paint\n  const ctrl = new AbortController(); // a named box\n  fetch(\"/u/\" + id, { signal: ctrl.signal })\n    .then((r) => r.json()) // shows the idea\n    .then(setUser)\n    .catch(() => {}); // shows the idea\n  return () => ctrl.abort();     // cancel last request\n}, [id]); // shows the idea" },
    { id: 33, level: "intermediate", q: "Error boundaries?", a: "An error boundary is a class with componentDidCatch that can show a fallback UI. They catch errors in child render, not in click handlers or async code.\n\nOne broken widget then does not blank the whole app.\n\nIn the code: Boundary stores err. getDerivedStateFromError sets err true. render shows Oops or children. It is a class because this API is still class-based.\n\nWrap unstable children, not the whole app, so you can recover a piece.", code: "class Boundary extends React.Component { // a blueprint\n  state = { err: false }; // one CSS rule\n  static getDerivedStateFromError() {\n    return { err: true };         // show fallback\n  }\n  render() {\n    return this.state.err ? <p>Oops</p> : this.props.children; // send this back\n  }\n}" },
    { id: 34, level: "intermediate", q: "What is lifting state vs a store?", a: "Lift state when a few nearby components share data. Use context or a store when many distant parts need it, or the rules are complex.\n\nStart simple. A counter does not need Redux.\n\nIn the code: Parent holds cart and setCart. ItemList receives both as props. Nearby kids share one cart.\n\nIf only two children need the data, lift it. Add a store later if the tree gets wide.", code: "function Parent() { // a reusable step\n  const [cart, setCart] = useState([]); // lift for nearby kids\n  return <ItemList cart={cart} setCart={setCart} />; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 35, level: "intermediate", q: "Redux in one minute?", a: "Redux keeps one store of state. You dispatch an action. A pure reducer returns the next state. React-Redux lets components subscribe.\n\nRedux Toolkit is the usual style today. An action is a description of what happened. A reducer is a function: (state, action) => nextState.\n\nIn the code: reducer returns { n: state.n + 1 } for type \"inc\". useReducer starts at { n: 0 }. dispatch({ type: \"inc\" }) asks for that update.\n\nUse a reducer when updates have names and depend on previous state.", code: "function reducer(state, action) { // a reusable step\n  if (action.type === \"inc\") return { n: state.n + 1 }; // send this back\n  return state; // send this back\n}\nconst [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, { n: 0 }); // a named box\ndispatch({ type: \"inc\" }); // one CSS rule" },
    { id: 36, level: "intermediate", q: "useReducer when?", a: "useReducer shines when many transitions depend on previous state in different ways. It is like a tiny Redux inside one component, or via context.\n\nWizards and complex forms fit this.\n\nIn the code: reducer toggles on for type \"toggle\". useReducer starts { on: false }. dispatch is how you send the named action.\n\nIf you have five related setX calls, a reducer is often clearer.", code: "function reducer(state, action) { // a reusable step\n  switch (action.type) {\n    case \"toggle\": return { on: !state.on }; // send this back\n    default: return state; // send this back\n  }\n}\nconst [s, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, { on: false }); // a named box" },
    { id: 37, level: "intermediate", q: "Custom hooks?", a: "A custom hook is a function starting with use that calls other hooks. Share useAuth or useDebounce without wrapping extra components. Return values, not usually JSX.\n\nCopy-pasting the same effect is a sign you want a hook.\n\nIn the code: useToggle holds on and returns [on, toggle]. Lamp uses it and renders a button. The hook has the logic. The component has the UI.\n\nName it useSomething so React’s hook rules apply.", code: "function useToggle(start = false) { // a reusable step\n  const [on, setOn] = useState(start); // remember a value\n  const toggle = () => setOn((x) => !x); // a named box\n  return [on, toggle];           // logic, not UI\n}\nfunction Lamp() { // a reusable step\n  const [on, toggle] = useToggle(); // a named box\n  return <button onClick={toggle}>{on ? \"On\" : \"Off\"}</button>; // when the user acts\n}" },
    { id: 38, level: "intermediate", q: "useId?", a: "useId gives a unique id that stays the same across server and client. Use it for htmlFor and aria-labelledby. Do not use it as a list key.\n\nLabels then point at the right input after hydration.\n\nIn the code: Field calls useId(). label htmlFor={id} matches input id={id}. Email is the visible text.\n\nNever use useId() as a map key. Keys should come from your data.", code: "function Field() { // a reusable step\n  const id = useId(); // a named box\n  return ( // send this back\n    <>\n      <label htmlFor={id}>Email</label>\n      <input id={id} />\n    </>\n  );\n}" },
    { id: 39, level: "intermediate", q: "forwardRef and useImperativeHandle?", a: "forwardRef lets a function component accept a ref. useImperativeHandle chooses what the parent is allowed to call. Use this rarely; props are usually enough.\n\nFocusing an inner input is a typical case.\n\nIn the code: Field is forwardRef. input is an inner ref. useImperativeHandle exposes { focus }. The input uses ref={input}. The parent can call ref.current.focus().\n\nDo not expose the whole DOM node if you only need focus.", code: "const Field = forwardRef(function Field(props, ref) { // a named box\n  const input = useRef(null); // box that does not redraw\n  useImperativeHandle(ref, () => ({ // shows the idea\n    focus: () => input.current.focus() // shows the idea\n  })); // shows the idea\n  return <input ref={input} />; // send this back\n}); // shows the idea" },
    { id: 40, level: "intermediate", q: "What is hydration?", a: "Hydration is when the browser already has HTML from the server, and React attaches event handlers instead of building from scratch. If the client HTML does not match (random ids, dates), you get warnings and extra work.\n\nNext.js and SSR depend on a match.\n\nIn the code: Clock uses useState(() => new Date().toString()). Server time and client time can differ, which is bad for hydration. The p shows t.\n\nDo not render Date.now() or Math.random() during SSR without a plan.", code: "function Clock() { // a reusable step\n  // bad for hydration: server time != client time\n  const [t] = useState(() => new Date().toString()); // remember a value\n  return <p>{t}</p>; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 41, level: "advanced", q: "What is Fiber?", a: "Fiber is React’s engine for splitting render work into small units. Work can pause, reuse, or abort. That enables concurrent rendering and priorities.\n\nYou do not call Fiber yourself. Your components become Fiber nodes behind the scenes.\n\nIn the code: App returns Home. The comments say React may pause rendering Home to keep typing smooth. There is no Fiber API in your file.\n\nYou opt into concurrent features with APIs like startTransition, not by calling Fiber.", code: "function App() { // a reusable step\n  // your components are Fiber nodes behind the scenes\n  return <Home />; // send this back\n}\n// React may pause rendering Home to keep typing smooth" },
    { id: 42, level: "advanced", q: "Concurrent rendering?", a: "Concurrent rendering means React can work on the next UI in the background. The current screen stays until the new one is ready. startTransition marks an update as not urgent.\n\nUse it for big lists while the user types.\n\nIn the code: q updates immediately in onChange. startTransition wraps setList(filter(...)). The input stays snappy. The list can wait.\n\nMark heavy filters as transitions. Keep the input update urgent.", code: "function Search() { // a reusable step\n  const [q, setQ] = useState(\"\"); // remember a value\n  const [list, setList] = useState([]); // remember a value\n  function onChange(e) { // when the user acts\n    setQ(e.target.value);                 // urgent: input\n    startTransition(() => setList(filter(e.target.value))); // shows the idea\n  }\n  return <input value={q} onChange={onChange} />; // when the user acts\n}" },
    { id: 43, level: "advanced", q: "startTransition vs debounce?", a: "Debounce waits to start the work at all. startTransition starts soon but says this paint can wait. The old UI stays until the heavy render is ready.\n\nThey solve different problems and can be combined.\n\nIn the code: setText(text) shows letters now. startTransition runs setResults(search(text)). The heavy list can wait. There is no timeout here.\n\nUse debounce to skip extra network calls. Use startTransition to keep typing smooth during a heavy render.", code: "function onType(text) { // a reusable step\n  setText(text); // show letters now\n  startTransition(() => { // shows the idea\n    setResults(search(text)); // heavy list can wait\n  }); // shows the idea\n}" },
    { id: 44, level: "advanced", q: "useTransition and useDeferredValue?", a: "useTransition wraps a setState as a transition and gives isPending. useDeferredValue lags a value so typing stays snappy while a list catches up.\n\nSearch UIs use both.\n\nIn the code: deferred is useDeferredValue(query). isPending comes from useTransition. The snippet shows a pending message and List query={deferred}. start is unused in the return, but it is how you wrap updates.\n\nShow a pending hint when isPending is true, so the user knows the list is catching up.", code: "function Page({ query }) { // a reusable step\n  const deferred = useDeferredValue(query); // lag the filter\n  const [isPending, start] = useTransition(); // a named box\n  return ( // send this back\n    <>\n      {isPending && <p>Updating…</p>}\n      <List query={deferred} />\n    </>\n  );\n}" },
    { id: 45, level: "advanced", q: "Suspense?", a: "Suspense lets a child say it is not ready, and shows a fallback. Lazy components and some data APIs use this. Error boundaries catch crashes. Suspense catches loading.\n\nUse it for code splitting and streaming.\n\nIn the code: Page is lazy(() => import(\"./Page\")). App wraps Page in Suspense with fallback Loading…. Until the import finishes, you see the fallback.\n\nWrap lazy() in Suspense or React has nowhere to show a loading state.", code: "const Page = lazy(() => import(\"./Page\")); // a named box\nfunction App() { // a reusable step\n  return ( // send this back\n    <Suspense fallback={<p>Loading…</p>}>\n      <Page />\n    </Suspense>\n  );\n}" },
    { id: 46, level: "advanced", q: "React.lazy and code splitting?", a: "lazy(() => import(\"./Page\")) downloads that file on demand. Wrap it in Suspense. Split big routes, not every tiny file.\n\nThe first visit then ships less JavaScript.\n\nIn the code: Chart is lazy-loaded. Dash wraps Chart in Suspense with a short fallback. Chart’s code loads when Dash renders it.\n\nDo not lazy-load a 10-line button. Split large pages and charts.", code: "const Chart = lazy(() => import(\"./Chart\")); // a named box\nfunction Dash() { // a reusable step\n  return ( // send this back\n    <Suspense fallback={<p>…</p>}>\n      <Chart />\n    </Suspense>\n  );\n}" },
    { id: 47, level: "advanced", q: "Why are render functions supposed to be pure?", a: "Render should be a pure description of UI. React may render extra times in Strict Mode and concurrent mode. Side effects in render become duplicate bugs.\n\nPut them in effects or event handlers. Drawing the picture should not also send mail.\n\nIn the code: fetch in the body is commented as BAD. useEffect fetches after paint when user.id changes. The p shows user.name.\n\nIf you fetch during render, Strict Mode will fetch twice in development.", code: "function Profile({ user }) { // a reusable step\n  // fetch(\"/x\");           // BAD: runs during render\n  useEffect(() => { // run after paint\n    fetch(\"/x/\" + user.id); // OK: after paint\n  }, [user.id]); // shows the idea\n  return <p>{user.name}</p>; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 48, level: "advanced", q: "Strict Mode double effects?", a: "In development, Strict Mode mounts, cleans up, and remounts to find missing cleanups. Write effects that can start and stop safely. This does not happen the same way in production.\n\nA missing cleanup then double-subscribes forever.\n\nIn the code: the effect calls listen(), then returns sub.stop(). That stop must be safe to run extra times. The deps are [].\n\nIf you see two subscriptions in dev, add a cleanup before you “fix” production.", code: "useEffect(() => { // run after paint\n  const sub = listen();      // start\n  return () => sub.stop();   // must be safe to run extra times\n}, []); // shows the idea" },
    { id: 49, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you optimize a slow list?", a: "Virtualize a huge list: only render what is on screen. Use stable keys, memoize rows, avoid heavy work in render. Do not put the whole list in a context that updates often.\n\nScroll stays smooth.\n\nIn the code: Row is memoized and shows item.name. List maps items with key={item.id}. Each row can skip if item is the same.\n\nmemo plus keys helps. For thousands of rows, also use a windowing library.", code: "const Row = memo(function Row({ item }) { // a named box\n  return <li>{item.name}</li>; // send this back\n}); // shows the idea\nfunction List({ items }) { // a reusable step\n  return items.map((item) => <Row key={item.id} item={item} />); // make a new list\n}" },
    { id: 50, level: "intermediate", q: "Why is creating a new object in render a problem with memo?", a: "style={{}} and onClick={() => {}} create a new object or function every render. memo sees “new props” even if they look the same.\n\nExtract a stable callback or memoize when it actually matters.\n\nIn the code: style is a constant outside the component. Item uses onSave and style. MemoItem is memo(Item). onSave must stay stable too for memo to help.\n\nMove static style objects out of render. Wrap handlers in useCallback if a memo child needs them.", code: "const style = { color: \"red\" }; // stable if outside\nfunction Item({ onSave }) { // a reusable step\n  return <button onClick={onSave} style={style}>Save</button>; // when the user acts\n}\nconst MemoItem = memo(Item); // a named box" },
    { id: 51, level: "beginner", q: "className vs class?", a: "class is a reserved word in JavaScript. JSX uses className for CSS classes. Labels use htmlFor instead of for.\n\nIt is just a naming rule.\n\nIn the code: the label has htmlFor=\"go\" and className=\"lbl\". The button has id=\"go\" and className=\"btn\". Clicking the label focuses the button.\n\nWrite className, not class, or the compiler will complain.", code: "function Btn() { // a reusable step\n  return ( // send this back\n    <label htmlFor=\"go\" className=\"lbl\">\n      Go\n      <button id=\"go\" className=\"btn\">OK</button>\n    </label>\n  );\n}" },
    { id: 52, level: "beginner", q: "How do you conditionally render?", a: "Use && for simple show, a ternary for if/else, or return early. Watch out: {count && <p/>} can show 0. Extract a variable if it gets messy.\n\nLoading and error screens use this all the time.\n\nIn the code: if n === 0, Box returns Empty. vip ? <Star /> : null shows a star only for vip. n > 0 && shows the count. Early return handles empty.\n\nPrefer n > 0 &&, not count &&, when count can be 0.", code: "function Box({ n, vip }) { // a reusable step\n  if (n === 0) return <p>Empty</p>;     // early return\n  return ( // send this back\n    <div>\n      {vip ? <Star /> : null}\n      {n > 0 && <p>{n} items</p>}\n    </div>\n  );\n}" },
    { id: 53, level: "beginner", q: "How do you show a list?", a: "Use array.map and give each item a key. Do not use map when you only need one element. This is the everyday list pattern for feeds, tables, and menus.\n\nKeys must be stable.\n\nIn the code: Names maps names to li with key={name}. The ul wraps the list. Each name is both key and text here.\n\nIf two names can be the same, use a real id as the key.", code: "function Names({ names }) { // a reusable step\n  return ( // send this back\n    <ul>\n      {names.map((name) => (\n        <li key={name}>{name}</li>\n      ))}\n    </ul>\n  );\n}" },
    { id: 54, level: "intermediate", q: "What is one-way data flow?", a: "One-way data flow means data goes down as props. Events go up as callbacks. That is easier to debug than two-way binding everywhere.\n\nYou can trace who changed a value.\n\nIn the code: Parent owns n and passes n and onAdd. Child shows n and calls onAdd on click. Parent’s setN runs. Data down, event up.\n\nDo not let Child edit Parent’s state except through a callback.", code: "function Parent() { // a reusable step\n  const [n, setN] = useState(0); // remember a value\n  return <Child n={n} onAdd={() => setN(n + 1)} />; // send this back\n}\nfunction Child({ n, onAdd }) { // a reusable step\n  return <button onClick={onAdd}>{n}</button>; // when the user acts\n}" },
    { id: 55, level: "intermediate", q: "derived state anti-pattern?", a: "Copying props into state is often wrong. You store a copy, then the prop changes, and the copy is stale. Compute during render instead, or reset with a key.\n\nSelected items and forms hit this a lot.\n\nIn the code: Price computes withTax = amount * 1.1 during render. It does not store withTax in state. The p shows withTax.\n\nIf amount changes, a copied state value would lag. Compute it.", code: "function Price({ amount }) { // a reusable step\n  const withTax = amount * 1.1; // compute, do not copy to state\n  return <p>{withTax}</p>; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 56, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you reset a component's state?", a: "Change a component’s key to start it fresh. React remounts and state goes back to the initial value. Common when the selected user id changes.\n\nForms then do not leak the last person.\n\nIn the code: Page renders Editor with key={userId}. A new id means a new Editor instance and new state. The comment states that.\n\nWhen a form should reset, key={id} is simpler than a pile of useEffects.", code: "// Example: How do you reset a component's state?\nfunction Page({ userId }) { // a reusable step\n  return <Editor key={userId} />; // new id → new state\n}" },
    { id: 57, level: "intermediate", q: "useLayoutEffect vs useEffect?", a: "useLayoutEffect runs after DOM updates but before the browser paints. Use it to measure layout and avoid a flicker. useEffect runs after paint and is the default.\n\nTooltips that must sit in the right place use layout effects.\n\nIn the code: useLayoutEffect reads box.current.offsetHeight, then setHeight(h). It runs before paint so the user does not see a jump. Deps are [].\n\nPrefer useEffect unless you are measuring the DOM.", code: "useLayoutEffect(() => { // shows the idea\n  const h = box.current.offsetHeight; // measure before paint\n  setHeight(h); // shows the idea\n}, []); // shows the idea" },
    { id: 58, level: "advanced", q: "What is batching in React 18?", a: "In React 18, updates inside timeouts, promises, and native events batch too. You get fewer renders and the same final state. You no longer need special tricks for that.\n\nTwo setStates become one paint.\n\nIn the code: onClick sets A and B together. setTimeout also sets A and B, and those batch in React 18. One render with both values.\n\nIf you still see two paints, check you are not on an old React.", code: "function onClick() { // when the user acts\n  setA(1); // shows the idea\n  setB(2); // one render with both\n}\nsetTimeout(() => { // run later\n  setA(3); // shows the idea\n  setB(4); // also batched in React 18\n}, 0); // shows the idea" },
    { id: 59, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you share logic without HOCs?", a: "Custom hooks share stateful logic. HOCs and render props still work but add wrapper layers. children composition is often enough for UI.\n\nYour tree stays shallower with hooks.\n\nIn the code: useOnline stores on, listens to online events, and cleans up. It returns on. Components call the hook instead of wrapping in withOnline.\n\nReuse logic with a hook. Reuse markup with children.", code: "function useOnline() { // a reusable step\n  const [on, setOn] = useState(true); // remember a value\n  useEffect(() => { // run after paint\n    const go = () => setOn(navigator.onLine); // a named box\n    window.addEventListener(\"online\", go); // when the user acts\n    return () => window.removeEventListener(\"online\", go); // send this back\n  }, []); // shows the idea\n  return on; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 60, level: "intermediate", q: "What is an HOC?", a: "A higher-order component is a function that takes a component and returns a new one with extra props. Older connect and withRouter were HOCs. Name them withXxx.\n\nHooks replaced many of these.\n\nIn the code: withTitle(Comp) returns Wrapped, which renders Comp with title=\"Hi\" and the rest of the props. Page is withTitle of a function that shows h1 title.\n\nIf you only need state, write a hook instead of an HOC.", code: "function withTitle(Comp) { // a reusable step\n  return function Wrapped(props) { // send this back\n    return <Comp title=\"Hi\" {...props} />; // send this back\n  };\n}\nconst Page = withTitle(function Page({ title }) { // a named box\n  return <h1>{title}</h1>; // send this back\n}); // shows the idea" },
    { id: 61, level: "beginner", q: "SPA vs MPA with React?", a: "An SPA loads one JS app and changes views in the browser. An MPA loads new HTML for each page. React can do both. Next.js adds server rendering.\n\nSpeed and SEO differ by choice.\n\nIn the code: page state is \"home\" or not. App shows Home or About. That is a tiny SPA with no URL yet.\n\nUse a router when the URL should match the view.", code: "function App() { // a reusable step\n  const [page, setPage] = useState(\"home\"); // remember a value\n  return page === \"home\" ? <Home /> : <About />; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 62, level: "intermediate", q: "CSR vs SSR vs SSG?", a: "CSR: the browser builds HTML from JS. SSR: the server builds HTML per request. SSG: HTML is built at compile time. Hydration attaches React on the client for all of these.\n\nFirst paint and SEO differ.\n\nIn the code: Page only returns h1 {title}. The same component can be CSR, SSR, or SSG depending on the framework. There is no fetch here.\n\nAsk your framework which of the three it uses for this route.", code: "function Page({ title }) { // a reusable step\n  // same component can be CSR, SSR, or SSG depending on the framework\n  return <h1>{title}</h1>; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 63, level: "intermediate", q: "Next.js app router idea?", a: "In the Next.js App Router, folders become routes. Server Components are the default. Layouts persist. You opt into client with \"use client\" for state and effects.\n\nUse this model if you use Next.\n\nIn the code: \"use client\" marks the file. Like uses useState and a button. That needs the client. Server files cannot call useState.\n\nPut \"use client\" on the smallest file that needs state, not on the whole tree.", code: "\"use client\"; // shows the idea\nfunction Like() { // a reusable step\n  const [n, setN] = useState(0); // needs client\n  return <button onClick={() => setN(n + 1)}>{n}</button>; // when the user acts\n}" },
    { id: 64, level: "advanced", q: "Server Components vs Client Components?", a: "Server components run on the server, can use secrets and databases, and send less JS. Client components handle state, effects, and browser APIs.\n\nYou cannot pass functions from server to client as props. Next.js apps use this split.\n\nIn the code: User is async, awaits db.user(id), and returns a p. There is no useState. That is a server component pattern.\n\nKeep secrets in server components. Pass plain data down to client children.", code: "// Server Component (no useState)\nasync function User({ id }) { // can wait inside\n  const user = await db.user(id); // server only\n  return <p>{user.name}</p>; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 65, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you do routing in CRA / Vite React?", a: "React Router maps URLs to components. Use BrowserRouter, Routes, Route, Link, useParams, useNavigate. Outlet is for nested layouts.\n\nUse Link, not a raw full-page <a>, for in-app moves.\n\nIn the code: Routes has / for Home and /u/:id for User. path and element pair a URL to a component. No Link is shown here.\n\nWrap the app in a router provider, or Routes will not work.", code: "function App() { // a reusable step\n  return ( // send this back\n    <Routes>\n      <Route path=\"/\" element={<Home />} />\n      <Route path=\"/u/:id\" element={<User />} />\n    </Routes>\n  );\n}" },
    { id: 66, level: "intermediate", q: "protected routes?", a: "A wrapper reads auth and redirects to login. A loader can also redirect. Never trust only the UI. The API must check tokens too.\n\nA hidden button is not security.\n\nIn the code: Private checks user. If missing, it renders Navigate to /login. Otherwise it returns children. That is a client-side gate.\n\nAlways verify on the server. This component only hides the page.", code: "function Private({ user, children }) { // a reusable step\n  if (!user) { // pick a path\n    return <Navigate to=\"/login\" />; // bounce\n  }\n  return children; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 67, level: "beginner", q: "Why should you not mutate state directly?", a: "React uses Object.is to see if state changed. Same array means maybe no update. Copy, then change the copy.\n\nOtherwise the screen will not refresh.\n\nIn the code: add uses setItems([...items, \"b\"]). items.push(\"b\") is commented as BAD because it is the same array. The button shows items.length.\n\nNever mutate state arrays with push or splice.", code: "function List() { // a reusable step\n  const [items, setItems] = useState([\"a\"]); // remember a value\n  function add() { // a reusable step\n    setItems([...items, \"b\"]); // new array\n    // items.push(\"b\");        // BAD: same array\n  }\n  return <button onClick={add}>{items.length}</button>; // when the user acts\n}" },
    { id: 68, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you update an object in state?", a: "Copy with spread, override the field. Use a functional update if it depends on previous. For nested objects, copy each level or use Immer.\n\nDo not mutate.\n\nIn the code: user has name and age. birthday uses setUser((u) => ({ ...u, age: u.age + 1 })). The button shows user.age.\n\nSpread is shallow. Nested objects still need their own copy.", code: "function Profile() { // a reusable step\n  const [user, setUser] = useState({ name: \"Ada\", age: 21 }); // remember a value\n  function birthday() { // a reusable step\n    setUser((u) => ({ ...u, age: u.age + 1 })); // one CSS rule\n  }\n  return <button onClick={birthday}>{user.age}</button>; // when the user acts\n}" },
    { id: 69, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you update an array in state?", a: "map to change one item, filter to remove, spread to add. Never push or splice the existing array. Todo lists use these recipes every day.\n\nEach update returns a new array.\n\nIn the code: toggle maps items and flips done for matching id. remove filters out that id. The button calls toggle(1). State starts with one todo.\n\nCopy the item with { ...t, done: !t.done }. Do not edit t in place.", code: "function Todos() { // a reusable step\n  const [items, setItems] = useState([{ id: 1, done: false }]); // remember a value\n  const toggle = (id) => // a named box\n    setItems(items.map((t) => (t.id === id ? { ...t, done: !t.done } : t))); // make a new list\n  const remove = (id) => setItems(items.filter((t) => t.id !== id)); // keep some items\n  return <button onClick={() => toggle(1)}>ok</button>; // when the user acts\n}" },
    { id: 70, level: "advanced", q: "What is the difference between element and instance?", a: "An element is a plain object describing type and props — what render returns. An instance is the running component with state. Fiber nodes track instances.\n\n<Box /> is a description, not the live box yet.\n\nIn the code: element is <Hello name=\"Ada\" />, a description object. Hello uses useState(0) on the instance. The p shows name and n.\n\nDo not store elements expecting them to keep state. State lives on the instance after mount.", code: "const element = <Hello name=\"Ada\" />; // description object\nfunction Hello({ name }) { // a reusable step\n  const [n] = useState(0);            // instance has state\n  return <p>{name}{n}</p>; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 71, level: "intermediate", q: "Why does React need a single root?", a: "createRoot(el).render(<App />) owns one DOM container. You can have more roots, but it is unusual. Portals still belong to a React tree.\n\nThat is where the app starts.\n\nIn the code: root is createRoot on #root. root.render(<App />) puts one tree in that node. The comment says portals still belong to this tree.\n\nCall createRoot once per container, not on every render.", code: "// Example: Why does React need a single root?\nconst root = createRoot(document.getElementById(\"root\")); // a named box\nroot.render(<App />); // one tree in that node\n// portals still belong to this tree" },
    { id: 72, level: "beginner", q: "What is the difference between React and ReactDOM?", a: "react is the component model. react-dom draws to a website. React Native draws to phones. Same idea, different host.\n\nImport from the right package.\n\nIn the code: useState comes from \"react\". createRoot comes from \"react-dom/client\". App uses useState. createRoot(...).render(<App />) mounts it.\n\nDo not import createRoot from \"react\". It lives in react-dom.", code: "import { useState } from \"react\"; // bring in a name\nimport { createRoot } from \"react-dom/client\"; // bring in a name\nfunction App() { // a reusable step\n  const [n] = useState(0); // remember a value\n  return <p>{n}</p>; // send this back\n}\ncreateRoot(document.getElementById(\"root\")).render(<App />); // shows the idea" },
    { id: 73, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you test a React component?", a: "React Testing Library: render, screen.getByRole, click like a user. Test what the user sees, not internal state names.\n\nTests then survive refactors.\n\nIn the code: Counter has n and a button that shows Count {n}. A test would click the button and expect Count 1. There is no test runner in the snippet.\n\nQuery by role and name, not by CSS class.", code: "function Counter() { // a reusable step\n  const [n, setN] = useState(0); // remember a value\n  return ( // send this back\n    <button onClick={() => setN(n + 1)}>\n      Count {n}\n    </button>\n  );\n}" },
    { id: 74, level: "intermediate", q: "What is the testing-library guiding principle?", a: "The more tests resemble real use, the more confidence they give. Prefer roles and labels over CSS class names.\n\nDo not test implementation trivia.\n\nIn the code: Search has a label htmlFor=\"q\" and input id=\"q\". A test can find the textbox by the name Search. The label is what users and tests share.\n\nIf you test class names, a redesign breaks every test.", code: "function Search() { // a reusable step\n  return ( // send this back\n    <>\n      <label htmlFor=\"q\">Search</label>\n      <input id=\"q\" />\n    </>\n  );\n}" },
    { id: 75, level: "advanced", q: "How does React handle accessibility?", a: "React does not make you accessible automatically. You still need semantic HTML, labels, keyboard support, and focus in modals. Use aria-* only when a native tag is not enough.\n\nReal users depend on this.\n\nIn the code: Field has label htmlFor=\"email\" and input id=\"email\" type=\"email\". That pairing is the basic accessible field. No extra ARIA is needed.\n\nPrefer a real label and a real input before you add ARIA.", code: "function Field() { // a reusable step\n  return ( // send this back\n    <>\n      <label htmlFor=\"email\">Email</label>\n      <input id=\"email\" type=\"email\" />\n    </>\n  );\n}" },
    { id: 76, level: "intermediate", q: "focus trap in a modal?", a: "Tab should cycle inside the modal. On close, return focus to the button that opened it. Use role=\"dialog\" and aria-modal. Portals are usual.\n\nKeyboard users then are not lost behind the overlay.\n\nIn the code: Modal has a ref on Close. useEffect focuses that button on mount. role=\"dialog\" and aria-modal=\"true\" mark the dialog. onClose is on the button.\n\nA full trap also handles Tab at the edges. This snippet only does initial focus.", code: "function Modal({ onClose }) { // a reusable step\n  const ref = useRef(null); // box that does not redraw\n  useEffect(() => { // run after paint\n    ref.current.focus();           // focus inside\n  }, []); // shows the idea\n  return ( // send this back\n    <div role=\"dialog\" aria-modal=\"true\">\n      <button ref={ref} onClick={onClose}>Close</button>\n    </div>\n  );\n}" },
    { id: 77, level: "intermediate", q: "CSS in React options?", a: "You can use CSS files, CSS modules, Tailwind, or CSS-in-JS. Modules and Tailwind reduce global name clashes. Inline styles cannot do hover or media queries well.\n\nTeams pick a system and stay consistent.\n\nIn the code: styles is imported from Btn.module.css. className={styles.btn} applies the hashed class. Save is the button text.\n\nIf two files both use .btn in global CSS, they can clash. Modules avoid that.", code: "import styles from \"./Btn.module.css\"; // bring in a name\nfunction Btn() { // a reusable step\n  return <button className={styles.btn}>Save</button>; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 78, level: "beginner", q: "How do you pass a CSS class?", a: "Use className, not class. Modules give you styles.btn. clsx helps with conditions.\n\nThis is everyday styling.\n\nIn the code: cls is \"btn btn-main\" if primary, else \"btn\". The button uses className={cls}. Go is the label.\n\nBuild the string, then pass one className. Do not pass class=.", code: "function Btn({ primary }) { // a reusable step\n  const cls = primary ? \"btn btn-main\" : \"btn\"; // a named box\n  return <button className={cls}>Go</button>; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 79, level: "intermediate", q: "What is a render prop?", a: "A render prop is a prop that is a function returning UI. The parent shares state, the child decides markup. Hooks replaced many of these cases.\n\nList libraries still use it. You will see it in older code.\n\nIn the code: Mouse stores x. onMouseMove sets x from e.clientX. {render(x)} lets the parent draw from x. Mouse owns the data. The parent owns the JSX.\n\nIf you only need the data, a hook is simpler than render={}.", code: "function Mouse({ render }) { // a reusable step\n  const [x, setX] = useState(0); // remember a value\n  return ( // send this back\n    <div onMouseMove={(e) => setX(e.clientX)}>\n      {render(x)}\n    </div>\n  );\n}" },
    { id: 80, level: "advanced", q: "Why can context cause performance issues?", a: "Any context value change re-renders all consumers. Split state vs dispatch, memoize the value, or use a store that subscribes to slices.\n\nHigh-frequency updates like mouse position are a bad fit.\n\nIn the code: Provider holds n and setN. value is useMemo(() => ({ n, setN }), [n]). C.Provider passes value. Children still re-render when n changes.\n\nMemoizing the object stops extra renders from a new { n, setN } identity when n did not change.", code: "const C = createContext(null); // a named box\nfunction Provider({ children }) { // a reusable step\n  const [n, setN] = useState(0); // remember a value\n  const value = useMemo(() => ({ n, setN }), [n]); // a named box\n  return <C.Provider value={value}>{children}</C.Provider>; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 81, level: "intermediate", q: "Zustand / Jotai vs Redux?", a: "Zustand and Jotai have smaller APIs and less boilerplate. Redux shines with DevTools, middleware, and large predictable graphs. Pick based on team and problem size.\n\nDo not default to the heaviest tool.\n\nIn the code: useCount is a tiny local store idea: n and inc. It is not Zustand. It shows the “small API” direction. inc calls setN(n + 1).\n\nStart with useState. Add a library when many distant components need the same data.", code: "function useCount() { // a reusable step\n  const [n, setN] = useState(0); // tiny local store idea\n  return { n, inc: () => setN(n + 1) }; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 82, level: "intermediate", q: "What is React Query used for?", a: "TanStack Query (React Query) manages server state: cache, retries, stale time, refetch, mutations. It is not a replacement for all UI state like “is the modal open.”\n\nLists from an API are the sweet spot.\n\nIn the code: User fetches in useEffect and setUser. The comment says Query libraries add cache on top of this idea. The p shows user?.name.\n\nUse Query when you refetch, cache, and share server data. Keep modal open in useState.", code: "function User({ id }) { // a reusable step\n  const [user, setUser] = useState(null); // remember a value\n  useEffect(() => { // run after paint\n    fetch(\"/u/\" + id).then((r) => r.json()).then(setUser); // ask the network\n  }, [id]); // Query libraries add cache on top of this idea\n  return <p>{user?.name}</p>; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 83, level: "advanced", q: "stale-while-revalidate?", a: "Stale-while-revalidate means show cached data immediately, fetch in the background, then update. The UI feels fast and still gets fresh data.\n\nReact Query uses this a lot. Repeat visits to a page feel instant.\n\nIn the code: cache is a Map. load returns cache.get(id) if present, while also starting fetch. Then it awaits fresh, stores it, and returns data. Cached users see old data first.\n\nReturn the cached value immediately, then update when the new response arrives.", code: "const cache = new Map(); // a named box\nasync function load(id) { // can wait inside\n  const fresh = fetch(\"/u/\" + id).then((r) => r.json()); // ask the network\n  if (cache.has(id)) return cache.get(id); // show old\n  const data = await fresh; // pause until ready\n  cache.set(id, data); // shows the idea\n  return data; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 84, level: "beginner", q: "What does 'lifting state' solve that context also solves?", a: "Both lifting state and context share data. Lifting stays explicit in props and is easier for small trees. Context hides the wiring for distant consumers.\n\nPick the simpler one first.\n\nIn the code: Parent holds theme and passes <Child theme={theme} />. That is lifting with visible props. No context yet.\n\nIf Child is five layers down and many siblings need theme, then context.", code: "function Parent() { // a reusable step\n  const [theme, setTheme] = useState(\"light\"); // remember a value\n  return <Child theme={theme} />; // lift: visible props\n}" },
    { id: 85, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you debounce an input in React?", a: "Keep the input state instant so letters appear now. Keep a second delayed value with a timeout in an effect. Clear the timeout on change and unmount.\n\nYou then do not fetch every key.\n\nIn the code: q updates on each keystroke. The effect sets debounced after 300ms and clears the timeout on cleanup. The input is controlled by q.\n\nSearch with debounced, not q, so requests wait until typing pauses.", code: "function Search() { // a reusable step\n  const [q, setQ] = useState(\"\"); // remember a value\n  const [debounced, setDebounced] = useState(\"\"); // remember a value\n  useEffect(() => { // run after paint\n    const id = setTimeout(() => setDebounced(q), 300); // run later\n    return () => clearTimeout(id); // send this back\n  }, [q]); // shows the idea\n  return <input value={q} onChange={(e) => setQ(e.target.value)} />; // when the user acts\n}" },
    { id: 86, level: "advanced", q: "Why not fetch in the component body?", a: "Render must stay pure. You would fetch every render, and extra times in Strict Mode. Use effects, handlers, or framework data APIs.\n\nOtherwise you can flood your own API.\n\nIn the code: fetch in the body is commented BAD. useEffect fetches \"/u/\" + id when id changes. The component returns null. Cleanup is not shown here.\n\nIf id is in the body fetch, every parent render would fetch again.", code: "function User({ id }) { // a reusable step\n  // fetch(\"/u/\" + id); // BAD in body\n  useEffect(() => { // run after paint\n    fetch(\"/u/\" + id);  // OK\n  }, [id]); // shows the idea\n  return null; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 87, level: "intermediate", q: "What is the children as a function pattern?", a: "Children as a function is like render props: children is a function. Useful for list libraries. Hooks are cleaner when you only need logic.\n\nYou will see {({ item }) => ...} in older APIs.\n\nIn the code: Each maps items and calls children(item) inside a div with key={item.id}. The parent passes a function as children. Each item gets custom markup.\n\nIf children is a function, call it. Do not try to render the function as text.", code: "function Each({ items, children }) { // a reusable step\n  return items.map((item) => ( // make a new list\n    <div key={item.id}>{children(item)}</div>\n  )); // shows the idea\n}" },
    { id: 88, level: "beginner", q: "How do you add an event listener?", a: "In JSX, onClick={handler} with camelCase. Pass a function. Do not call it immediately unless you wrap () => handler(id).\n\nThat is the everyday event pattern.\n\nIn the code: onClick logs \"hi\". The button uses onClick={onClick}, not onClick={onClick()}. Go is the label.\n\nonClick={onClick()} would run on every render and pass undefined as the handler.", code: "function Btn() { // a reusable step\n  function onClick() { // when the user acts\n    console.log(\"hi\"); // print to see it\n  }\n  return <button onClick={onClick}>Go</button>; // when the user acts\n}" },
    { id: 89, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you pass arguments to an event handler?", a: "Wrap: () => deleteItem(id). Or bind. Avoid huge extra work in that closure on giant lists unless needed.\n\nLists of buttons need the id in the handler.\n\nIn the code: Row takes id and onDelete. The button calls () => onDelete(id). Delete is the label. Each row closes over its own id.\n\nDo not write onClick={onDelete(id)} without an arrow, or delete runs during render.", code: "function Row({ id, onDelete }) { // a reusable step\n  return ( // send this back\n    <button onClick={() => onDelete(id)}>\n      Delete\n    </button>\n  );\n}" },
    { id: 90, level: "advanced", q: "What is reconciliation's O(n) heuristic?", a: "React compares trees level by level, not a general tree-diff of all shapes. Different types remount. Keys guide list matching.\n\nThat is fast enough for UI. Use keys and stable types.\n\nIn the code: List maps items to Row with key={item.id}. The comment says keys make matching work. item is passed as a prop.\n\nWithout keys, React may reuse the wrong row when the list shifts.", code: "function List({ items }) { // a reusable step\n  return items.map((item) => ( // make a new list\n    <Row key={item.id} item={item} /> // keys make O(n) matching work\n  )); // shows the idea\n}" },
    { id: 91, level: "intermediate", q: "Why did my child remount when the parent type changed?", a: "If the parent element type changes (div vs span, A vs B), the subtree is thrown away. Keep types stable. Use CSS for layout changes when you can.\n\nThat is why a form can “reset itself.”\n\nIn the code: Wrap picks Tag = section or div from fancy. return <Tag>{children}</Tag>. A type change remounts children. children’s state is lost.\n\nIf you only need a class change, keep the same tag.", code: "function Wrap({ fancy, children }) { // a reusable step\n  const Tag = fancy ? \"section\" : \"div\"; // type change remounts\n  return <Tag>{children}</Tag>; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 92, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you share a WebSocket in React?", a: "Open one connection: module singleton, context, or a hook that counts subscribers. Dispatch messages into state. Close when the last consumer unmounts.\n\nOtherwise you open 20 sockets.\n\nIn the code: socket is one shared object with send. Chat’s effect sends \"hi\" on mount. The p says chat. Every Chat instance shares the same socket.\n\nDo not create a new WebSocket inside each component body.", code: "const socket = { send(msg) { console.log(msg); } }; // one shared object\nfunction Chat() { // a reusable step\n  useEffect(() => { // run after paint\n    socket.send(\"hi\"); // shows the idea\n  }, []); // shows the idea\n  return <p>chat</p>; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 93, level: "advanced", q: "useSyncExternalStore?", a: "useSyncExternalStore reads a consistent snapshot for concurrent rendering. Used for Redux and some browser APIs. getSnapshot should not return a new object every time unless cached.\n\nOtherwise tearing can show mixed old/new data.\n\nIn the code: useOnline subscribes to window online events and returns navigator.onLine. The subscribe function adds a listener and returns a remove function.\n\nReturn a stable snapshot. A new object every getSnapshot will loop renders.", code: "function useOnline() { // a reusable step\n  return useSyncExternalStore( // send this back\n    (cb) => { // shows the idea\n      window.addEventListener(\"online\", cb); // when the user acts\n      return () => window.removeEventListener(\"online\", cb); // send this back\n    },\n    () => navigator.onLine // shows the idea\n  );\n}" },
    { id: 94, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you handle authentication UI?", a: "Store the user in context or a query. Gate routes. Do not trust the client: APIs must check tokens. httpOnly cookies are a safer place for refresh tokens.\n\nA hidden button is not your only security.\n\nIn the code: Auth is a context. App provides user. It shows Home or Login. That is UI gating only.\n\nAlways check the session on the server for private data.", code: "const Auth = createContext(null); // a named box\nfunction App({ user }) { // a reusable step\n  return ( // send this back\n    <Auth.Provider value={user}>\n      {user ? <Home /> : <Login />}\n    </Auth.Provider>\n  );\n}" },
    { id: 95, level: "beginner", q: "What is the difference between a library and a framework here?", a: "React is a UI library: components and state. Next and Remix are frameworks: routing, bundling, data, conventions. You often use both layers.\n\nReact draws UI. A framework fills in the rest of the app.\n\nIn the code: App returns an h1. The comment says library: just UI. There is no router in the snippet.\n\nSay “React is a UI library” unless you are talking about a framework on top.", code: "// Example: What is the difference between a library and a framework here?\nfunction App() { // a reusable step\n  return <h1>React draws this</h1>; // library: just UI\n}" },
    { id: 96, level: "advanced", q: "What causes 'too many re-renders'?", a: "Too many re-renders often means calling setState during render with no guard, or an effect that always sets state. Move updates to handlers, or gate the effect.\n\nThe app freezes in a loop.\n\nIn the code: Bad has n. setN(n + 1) in the body is commented BAD. The button correctly sets state on click. The label shows n.\n\nIf you set state during render, React renders again, which sets state again.", code: "function Bad() { // a reusable step\n  const [n, setN] = useState(0); // remember a value\n  // setN(n + 1); // BAD: render → setState → render\n  return <button onClick={() => setN(n + 1)}>{n}</button>; // when the user acts\n}" },
    { id: 97, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you type React with TypeScript?", a: "Type props as a type or interface. useState<User | null>(null). Events like ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>. children is often React.ReactNode. FC is optional.\n\nSafer props catch missing fields in the editor.\n\nIn the code: Props has title and onClose. Modal destructures them with : Props. The button shows title and calls onClose. Types stay on the function argument.\n\nDo not use FC unless your team already does. A Props type is enough.", code: "type Props = { title: string; onClose: () => void }; // describe the shape\nfunction Modal({ title, onClose }: Props) { // a reusable step\n  return <button onClick={onClose}>{title}</button>; // when the user acts\n}" },
    { id: 98, level: "intermediate", q: "What is the difference between ReactNode and ReactElement?", a: "ReactElement is a created element object. ReactNode is anything renderable: element, string, number, null, fragments, arrays. props.children is usually ReactNode.\n\nDo not type children too tightly.\n\nIn the code: Props children is React.ReactNode. Wrap puts children in a div. A string or an element is ok. That matches how people use wrappers.\n\nIf you type children as ReactElement, passing \"hello\" fails.", code: "type Props = { children: React.ReactNode }; // describe the shape\nfunction Wrap({ children }: Props) { // a reusable step\n  return <div>{children}</div>; // string or element is ok\n}" },
    { id: 99, level: "advanced", q: "How would you design a reusable modal?", a: "A reusable modal portals to document.body, traps focus, closes on ESC and overlay click, and has labels. Controlled open prop. Return focus to the opener.\n\nKeep styling flexible via children. Modals are hard for accessibility.\n\nIn the code: if !open, return null. createPortal draws a dialog with aria-modal. Close calls onClose. children are the body. The portal target is document.body.\n\nDo not ship a modal without focus and ESC. This snippet is the skeleton, not the full trap.", code: "function Modal({ open, onClose, children }) { // a reusable step\n  if (!open) return null; // send this back\n  return createPortal( // send this back\n    <div role=\"dialog\" aria-modal=\"true\">\n      <button onClick={onClose}>Close</button>\n      {children}\n    </div>,\n    document.body // shows the idea\n  );\n}" },
    { id: 100, level: "advanced", q: "How do you talk through a React interview task?", a: "On a coding task, first list UI states: loading, error, empty, success. Agree on data shape and clicks. Split components by job. Lift state only as needed.\n\nMention keys and labels, then extra polish if they ask. Fancy hooks are optional on a short task.\n\nIn the code: Users branches on status and users.length. Loading, error, and empty each return a p. Success maps users with key={u.id}. That is the state list in JSX.\n\nSketch those four states before you write fetch.", code: "function Users({ status, users }) { // a reusable step\n  if (status === \"loading\") return <p>Loading…</p>; // send this back\n  if (status === \"error\") return <p>Try again</p>; // send this back\n  if (users.length === 0) return <p>No users</p>; // send this back\n  return <ul>{users.map((u) => <li key={u.id}>{u.name}</li>)}</ul>; // make a new list\n}" }
  ]
};
